  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nToday and tonight: A weak boundary has advanced through a portion of\nthe local area with some locations across west-central and north- \ncentral Ohio dipping into the 60s this morning. To the south, a warm \nmuggy air mass remains. This air mass will shift back to the north \ntoday as low pressure deepens over the central Plains. Southwesterly \nsurface flow quickly pushes the stalled boundary northward. This will\nprovide a warm and humid Sunday afternoon and evening with \ntemperatures climbing into the upper 80s and lower 90s. Heat indices \nwill range between 95 and 100 degrees. For the first time in the last\nfew days, thunderstorms are not expected during the afternoon and \nevening. However, thunderstorms will form along the front to the \nnorth of the local area late in the evening. As these thunderstorms \norganize into a complex, various CAMs indicate that it will move \nsouth and southeast across northwest Ohio and northeast Indiana\nthrough the overnight. This may eventually bring a damaging wind \nthreat through a portion of the area late in the overnight into early\nMonday morning. For now, much of the area is in a level 1 of 5\nMarginal threat for severe storms from the SPC. Decreasing wind \nshear to the south of the thunderstorm complex supports additional \nweakening as the leading outflow pushes away from the thunderstorms\nMonday morning.  \n\nMonday and Monday night: Depending on the ultimate progress of the\noutflow boundary in the morning, a lull in thunderstorm activity\ncould persist well into the afternoon as the air mass recovers from a\nremnant cold pool. This is expected to occur quite efficiently,\nwith steep mid-level lapse rates and potent surface moisture\nsupporting CAPE values between 3000-4000 J/kg. Wind shear is a bit\nstronger Monday evening, with westerly 0-3km shear vectors around 30\nknots. An additional thunderstorm complex is expected to form along\nthe stalled boundary to the north. The exact location of the\ndevelopment will greatly change the specific impacts across the \nlocal area. These details may not be known until Monday afternoon as \nthe air mass recovery begins to result in new thunderstorms. For \nnow, nearly the entire local area is currently in a level 2 of 5 \nSlight risk from the SPC. With increasing low-level flow Monday \nnight, thunderstorm activity is likely to be maintained into the \novernight hours. If multiple rounds of thunderstorms occur, flash \nflooding will be likely in those areas.\n\nTuesday: By now, multiple rounds of thunderstorms will have been\npossible across the local area, complicating the forecast going into\nTuesday. Typically in these scenarios, the outflow boundaries tend to\ndrift farther south than the potential recovery in the afternoon.\nHowever, on Tuesday, a shortwave, embedded within upper level \nnorthwesterly flow, slides southeastward through the Great Lakes. In\nthe low levels, persistent southwesterly flow will continue to feed\ndeep moisture northward into the local area. Depending on the\nevolution of thunderstorms overnight Monday into Tuesday morning,\nthese is plenty of support for additional rounds of thunderstorms\nthroughout the day on Tuesday. Damaging winds could still be a \nthreat across the area, but depending on the alignment of the outflow\nboundaries and the associated thunderstorms, flash flooding may\nbecome the main concern\n\nWednesday through Friday: There is a growing consensus across the\nlong range models that the bulk of the activity will shift southward\nWednesday and then again on Thursday. This would focus locally heavy\nrainfall threats across southern Ohio, southeast Indiana, and\nnorthern Kentucky. The ridge attempts to push northward again on\nFriday, bringing a shortwave through the Great Lakes. Confidence is\nmuch lower on the exact placement of this feature.",
